<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saulteaux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Plains Ojibwe, Nakawē</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saulteaux are a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> people, named by the French after the rapids of Sault Ste. Marie. In the 18th and 19th centuries many moved west into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, adopting horses and bison hunting as the Plains Ojibwe and allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a>. Many Saulteaux First Nations signed the numbered treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saulteaux.jpg" alt="Saulteaux" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DarrenBaker · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anishinaabe-Anishinini_Distribution_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
